In article <39088604.9B8ACA4E@aa.washington.edu>, Raymond Golingo <golingo@aa.washington.edu> writes:
:I am looking for a function for dec alphas that allocates memory.  I
:tried memalloc (works on other computer systems), but it failed.  Does
:anyone know the function???

  There is no memory allocation on Alpha, as Alpha is hardware and the
  physical and virtual memory allocation functions are generally
  provided by and controlled by the operating system software that is
  running on the hardware.  Three common operating systems on Alpha
  hardware are OpenVMS Alpha, Tru64 UNIX on Alpha, and Linux on Alpha.
  (There are others...)

  When posting, please include the version and the platform and some
  general details -- and in cases such as this, the language or the
  environment or the command(s) involved.  I am guessing that this is 
  a C programming question, and thus the memory allocation is standard 
  across all ANSI C compilers.  If this is not C, or involves something 
  specific to a particular platform or particular language, you will 
  want to provide further information -- I don't immediately recognize 
  a function called "memalloc"...

  The usual C virtual memory allocation function is called "malloc", 
  and the malloc call is available on OpenVMS Alpha, on Tru64 UNIX on 
  Alpha, and on Linux on Alpha.


