Are these machines very different from VAX-11s? Otherwise the DEFBOO.COM
file is just the default boot commandfile. At the prompt, you can boot
from anything by just giving the right argument to the BOOT command.
Any three letter combination is used as the first half of the command
filename, the second part being BOO.CMD. DEFBOO.COM is just a copy
of one of the other command files.

RASBOO.COM was, I believe, the standalone boot for RA disks on UDA-50
for the VAX-11, for instance. Multiused was probably RAMBOO.COM, but
my memory is fading...

So, you could just say "BOOT RAS" if you wanted to. "BOOT" without
any argument took DEFBOO.COM, and if the system was setup to boot
at powerup, this is what was run then too.

