Max,

Some things to consider...

1. The voltage regulator modules (VRM) in a PC5500 are held in by
little plastic clips.  They are easily removed and replaced, but
replacement is only part of the story.

2. Celeron CPUs 366Mhz (I think) and up are all Socket 370 PPGA
processors, not Slot 1.  To use one, you need a "Slotket", an adapter
with a socket for a PPGA Celeron and a Slot 1 that slides into the
motherboard.  Most slotkets handle any voltage conversion required, so
changing the VRM is not necessary.  A good slotket will also have
jumpers or switches to allow you to set the Celeron's clock multiplier
independent of the settings on the motherboard.

3. Celerons are not certified or qualified or whatever by Intel to run
in a multi-processor setup.  But some people have reported success
anyway.  You are on your own with dual Celerons.

That about covers it... Ben Myers

