  -- and there may well be some of these -- are likely mine...


 --------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
   Hoff (Stephen) Hoffman   OpenVMS Engineering   hoffman#xdelta.zko.dec.com




BOOT

Boots the system. During the installation procedure use the abbreviation,
B, for the BOOT command. The following example uses the boot command
procedure DEFBOO.COM to boot the system:

  >>> B


ENABLE AUTO BOOT
DISABLE AUTO BOOT

In combination with AUTO RESTART, determines how the system reacts
after one of the following events:

�   The system is initialized.

�   A power failure occurs.

�   An error halt after a restart attempt fails.

For example, the following commands cause the system to automatically
reboot after any of the events listed above:

  >>> DISABLE AUTO RESTART
  >>> ENABLE AUTO BOOT

The following command prevents the system from automatically rebooting
after any of the events listed above:

  >>> DISABLE AUTO BOOT


ENABLE AUTO POWERON
DISABLE AUTO POWERON

Determines what happens when power is restored after a power failure. For
example, the following command automatically applies power to the CPU
when power is restored after a power failure.

  >>> ENABLE AUTO POWERON

  The following command prevents the automatic application of power to the
  CPU when power is restored after a power failure:

  >>> DISABLE AUTO POWERON



CONTINUE

Resumes execution of macro instructions and switches the console back to
program mode.


DEPOSIT

Puts a value in the specified register or memory location.


EXAMINE

Displays the contents of a specified address.


INITIALIZE

Sets selected and available CPUs or memory to a defined initial state.


HALT

You guessed it...


EXIT

Exits the VAX console, gets to the Pro prompt...  (Use RUN CONTROL
to get from the Pro prompt back to the VAX console prompt.)



-- Basic Bootstrap Processing --

DEFBOO.COM is used by default, unless another file is used based on an
explicit BOOT command such as (to bootstrap off BDA unit 14, root (SYS5]):

  BOOT BDA14 /R5:50000000

This example triggers BDABOO.COM, and passes in a value via R5...

By default, DEFBOO.COM contains a BOOT command that triggers activation
of another bootstrap file, such as BDABOO.COM (default bootstrap), 
BDAGEN.COM (conversational bootstrap), and BDAXDT.COM (XDELTA bootstrap).
Each of these really only differs by the contents of R5 -- the rest of
these sets of three files is usually the same...

You get to modify the files for whichever disk -- probably either BDA
or UDA, depending on your disk controller, since you indicate this is
not a CI system, and that you have a DSA-series disk controller.  (The
KDB50 on the BI, or the UDA50 on the Unibus -- the former is a rather
better and preferable configuration than the latter, this for various 
reasons...)


-- Basic contents of the console bootstrap file ---

R1
  <31:06>    Must be zero    No meaning.

  <05:04>    0-3 (hex)       The VAXBI that has the adapter (CIBCI or 
                             CIBCA, etc) that accesses the system disk.

  <03:00>    0-F (hex)       The VAXBI node number of the adapter.

R2
  CI:
    <31:16>  Must be zero    No meaning.

    <15:08>  0-F (hex)       Node number of the second HSC if the boot 
                             device is dual-ported. If one of the HSCs 
                             is node 0, that node must be specified as 
                             the lowest byte. For example, 0100 means 
                             boot from either HSC 0 or 1, while 0001 
                             means boot from HSC 1 only.

    <07:00>  0-F (hex)       Node number of the first HSC.

  UDA:
    <17:00>  3F468 (hex)     UNIBUS address of the device's Control and 
                             Status Register (CSR).  (The value 3F468 is 
                             valid if you are booting off the first or 
                             only UDA on this UNIBUS.)  If you need to 
                             boot off another UDA, you'll need to use
                             the CSR for the target UDA.

  BDA:
    <31:06>  Must be zero    No meaning.


R3
  <31:00>                    Unit number of the drive that holds the system 
                             disk (expressed in decimal).  (Default case...
			     R3 is also involved when volume shadowing is
                             configuration, and can have differing values.)

R4
  <31:00>                    Secondary boot block, in hexadecimal.  Used only 
                             if Bit 3 is set in R5, and only needed if the
                             primary boot block is unavailable.

R5
  <31:00>                    See OpenVMS FAQ

  --


The usual sequence of bootstrap commands looks something like this:

>>> POWER ON
>>> @SYSINIT


The usual sequence of setup commands (needed once) looks something like this:

>>> ENABLE AUTO POWERON
>>> ENABLE AUTO BOOT
>>> ENABLE AUTO RESTART


Once the settings and the commands and DEFBOO (and the corresponding BDABOO
or UDABOO) are all in place, powering up the box will directly bootstrap 
the target disk...




