In article <PcEH4.246$hh6.7511@news2-win.server.ntlworld.com>, "Kevin Murrell" <kevinm@xpuppy.freeserve.co.uk> writes:
:Can anyone let me know the part number for the DSSI to SCSI converter.
:IIRC it plugs into the DSSI socket on my VS3300   I need to be able to
:support SCSI disks and CDROM, and be able to boot from them.

  I am aware of no "VS3300" system, nor anything known as a "VAXstation 
  3300" system.  I will assume this is a VAXserver 3300, or potentially 
  a MicroVAX 3300.  (Please do not abbreviate system names -- the names
  are often close enough that this can and often does lead to confusion.)
  Also please post the OpenVMS (OpenVMS VAX) version involved...

  Some of the approaches available:

    o HSD05 or HSD10 -- one of the variants of these widgets plugs into 
      the Q-bus, while the others plug into the StorageWorks shelf...

      These can be connected to the integrated controller present in
      the KA640 CPU used in the MicroVAX 3300 and VAXserver 3300, and
      to other CPUs with integrated DSSI, and can also be connected to 
      the KFQSA Q-bus to DSSI adapter module.

    o Third-party Q-bus to SCSI adapter.  Those that correctly emulate
      DU-class disks will likely be bootable, assuming various factors,
      including such things as console support and OpenVMS support for 
      the particular disk capacity.  (OpenVMS prior to V6, for instance,
      has limits in this area.  See the FAQ for details.  This is why
      including the OpenVMS version number in the question is useful.)

    o KZQSA Q-bus to (specific) SCSI adapter.  For use with specific 
      tapes and CD-ROM devices only.  Not supported for faster SCSI
      widgets such as magnetic disks, nor for random SCSI peripherals.

    o Cluster with or upgrade to a MicroVAX or VAXstation series
      system, or (potentially) to an Alpha system.  In addition to 
      native SCSI access, this also greatly improves the performance 
      of the configuration.  (The MicroVAX 3300 is very old and very 
      slow, and less-old and less-slow native-SCSI OpenVMS systems 
      are readily available...)

  Are you using the Q-bus for anything odd -- for any Q-bus I/O widgets 
  other than the typical storage and networking?  If not, I'd tend to
  recommend simply upgrading the whole box to a new one...
      

 --------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
   Hoff (Stephen) Hoffman   OpenVMS Engineering   hoffman#xdelta.zko.dec.com




