in article 38fcdd4d.30216659@news.ma.ultranet.com, Ben Myers at
benmyers@ultranet.com on 4/18/00 6:18 PM wrote something like:

> I have an older DEClaser 1152 that has hardly ever been used.  Its
> page count is just over 11,000.  It prints its startup page
> flawlessly.  I have reset it to the factory defaults, and I've tried
> to use it connected to an Intel x86 computer running Windows 95 with
> the DEClaser 1152 PostScript drivers installed.  Whether I try to
> print the Windows 95 test page or some other printed page, the results
> are the same.  The Windows 95 printer queue shows a document being
> printed for a few seconds, then the printer queue is empty.  But the
> printer does not print anything and its data light never flashes to
> indicate data coming from the computer.
> 
> The computer, set up with the right print drivers, and printer cable
> work OK together with other laser printers.  Does this sound like a
> broken printer or simply a printer that is set up incorrectly?  How
> can I figure out?  Can someone provide a written description of how to
> operate the panel buttons to select various options?  So far, the
> operation of the panel buttons is bizarre, to say the least.  But, at
> least I did manage to get the factory defaults reset.
> 
> Any help would be appreciated.  Thanks... Ben Myers

    I haven't been near an 1152 in about a year or two now, but IIRC: 1152
does not auto-select the input port, you must go into the menu and select
the parallel port (port "B" ?) and hit enter so that an * appears next to
it. That should work, the self-test page should tell you what port is
selected and what other options have you. There *may* be a way to turn on
port auto-select, but I honestly don't remember if/how to do it. If you're
only using it with Windoze then that shouldn't matter for you.

    Panel Operations generally follow:

1.) take printer off-line
2.) hit menu to find the option you want, then it's either select or "+" to
view that particular options' settings and/or submenus. Using the arrow key
to move to the option you want, you must then hit enter (or select?) to
"activate" that value. (An * should appear I think)
3.) put printer online


    Chris




