In article <38FD79E0.12A9B1AF@bessel.mpip-mainz.mpg.de>, Matthias Koch <koch@bessel.mpip-mainz.mpg.de> writes:

  Based solely on the switch over to a discussion of the Multia platform, 
  I have changed the subject line for this particular discussion thread.

  Please don't get the AlphaStation 500 firmware information mixed up 
  with the Multia firmware information, as these two are completely 
  different system platforms, and as what you are likely trying to do
  with the Multia (booting OpenVMS?) is not officially supported...

:Hoff Hoffman wrote:
:
:>   Load a firmware floppy (see the FAQ) into the DVA0: floppy drive.
:
:I prepared a firmware floppy in the way described on the compaq
:homepage, but this did not work. Can I really boot directly from the
:firmware floppy? The multia needs a different one before the firmware
:can be loaded.

  Please: "Did not work" is not a particularly useful problem description.
  This phrase is the scourge of support staffs the world over, and is a
  phrase that -- when used in isolation -- I would encourage you to quickly
  and permanently purge from your vocabulary...  (No offense is intended!)

  For the Multia system, please acquire and exactly follow the directions 
  provided in the OpenVMS Multia kit located at the Freeware website:

    http://www.openvms.digital.com/freeware/multia/

  If the OpenVMS installation description at this URL do not work (and
  you will have to look *in* the kit to get the full text), you will want 
  to provide details on exactly what you did and exactly what happened 
  and exactly what error or informational message(s) (if any) were 
  displayed and exactly what firmware you used, etc.  

:The only upgrade procedure documented is the regular one on an already
:working system.

  The Multia is not a system that is officially supported for use with 
  OpenVMS.  Things like the SRM firmware -- firmware that is not needed
  for the target operating system for the Multia platform -- and OpenVMS
  will thus accordingly be poorly documented (or entirely undocumented)
  for the Multia platform.

  I would also encourage anyone working with OpenVMS Alpha systems to
  get onto the CD-ROM firmware distribution.  It is cheap, and it makes
  dealing with the firmware in these situations (save for OpenVMS on the 
  Multia or other unsupported platforms, of course) far easier...

