> SPAM:   spamrecycle@ChooseYourmail.com  uce@ftc.gov     postmaster@127.0.0.1

 1. Buy vga to BNC monitor cable.
 2. Connect cable.
 3. Make sure video card is supplying proper syncs 
    (you can find basic specs at
http://monitorworld.com/Monitors/dec/VRT19HA.html) 


Monitor should now work fine on PC, just don't try to switch
resolutions.
You don't need any special 'converters' if you don't need to switch 
resolutions on the fly.  You might need a sync-on-green converter, 
however, if your video card will not supply it.  Most Matrox cards can 
be told to do this.  If your card can not supply sync-on-green, you can 
get a converter for ~$30-50.

You can find more info on using fixed-frequency monitors on PCs at 
http://cvs.anu.edu.au/monitorconversion.  More detailed specs on the 
VRT19-HA can be found at 

http://www.geocities.com/SiliconValley/Foothills/4467/fixedsync.html

This page has alot of useful information, including adjusting XFee86, 
Win95, and WinNT settings.


Good Luck

-Eric Christy



