Steve & Steve,

I think you (Steve Lionel) are correct in your understanding.  Most
(all but the earliest) Socket 5/7 Pentium OverDrives are based on the
MMX core design with supplementary external voltage regulation
circuits.  Intel never certified the MMX core for multi-processor use,
and may well have left some multi-processor functions out of the chip
core.  I once had a little sheet of paper that deciphered the markings
on the bottom of an Intel Pentium Socket 5/7 chip.  The markings
include voltage and MP use.

The foregoing explains the excessively high street price of Intel P200
non-MMX chips up until a couple of months ago.  Many companies wanted
to give their Pentium-based servers (and many desktops) one last shot
with performance upgrades.  Now, Pentium 200s are more affordable,
maybe in the $75 range when I last looked on eBay.

If you (Steve Craft) want to work a swap with a few bucks going one
way or the other, I still have some genuine DEC-branded P166s with
heat sinks, made for Prioris/Celebris upgrades.  Except that you'd
then need a 54-24002 voltage regulator, currently scarce as hen's
teeth.

All these Steve's remind me of the Monty Python skit with the Bruces.

... Ben Myers

