}no it's not. if someone sent you a defective drive, then you would receive a defective
}drive.

However, just because it arrived in a defective state does not
mean it was defective when sent.

Things are damaged in shipping all the time. Even non-mechancal
things. A few months ago, I had a book damaged in shipping (it
had a couple of chunks taken out of it). Did I make moronic statemnts
like, "It arrived damaged therefore you must have sent me a damaged
book"? Of course not - I'm not an idiot. I am well aware that the
package did not teleport from the sender to my mailbox. There was
a considerable amount of stuff that happened in between its being
sent and its arrival (some of which was obviously rather unpleasant).

--- Carl



