I have been watching this thread develop and like many
who have posted, I am somewhat overwhelmed by the noise
volume of some of these posts.  Under most circumstances
I would be inclined to dismiss this whole matter, except,
I have had dealings with the "circuitsurgeon" and since
Ft Wayne, Indiana is home to both "circuitsurgeon and 
myself, honesty compels me to offer the following:


My encounter with the subject, ciruitsurgeon, had nothing
to do with the internet or decus (although I have been a
lurking decus member for not quite 15 years) but rather
a local "swap & shop" weekly called "The Peddler's Post".
The incident recounted below occurred 1-2 years ago 
during warmer months.  I have heard the criticism of
Dan, the original poster, and only can state it's not
worth the effort to dig out the exact dates.

We had an old LN03 attached to an RS/6000 serial port 
printing one particular job and as LN03's are want to do,
the fuser unit decided it no longer liked us.  When 
reading the "Peddler's Post" what should I find, but
an ad for a LN03 listed at $100.00.  The seller was
Mr Gil, also dba as, you guessed it, circuitsurgeon.

I called the number listed, made arrangements to drive
to circuitsurgeon's house about 2-3 miles (6-8 min from
my office.  I was told that he had just removed the
LN03 from a working system and it was in good working
order. (Although I seemed to recall he had some difficulty
finding the (a) feed tray for paper in.  This struck
me as a bit strange since he had just removed it
from a working system (why wasn't the tray 
immediately at hand?)

Now accompanying me on this trip was my programmer of
many years and lady friend.  She definitely did not
like the place or the seller and said that he "gave
her the creeps".  My recollection is that
after picking up the LN03 we went directly to church
as is our practice on Thursday evenings.  Our parish
church is approximately halfway between the
circuitsurgeon's house and my office.  From the church
we proceeded back to the office and went to install
our bargain LN03.  

Guess what?  I have messed with LN03's before, but
the printer which we transported very carefully and
without incident never produced so much as (1) copy.
The next weekend I tried swapping trays, etc but to
no avail.  We talked it over and based upon the
atmosphere at the time of purchase we vowed never
to buy any item locally without insisting that
the seller demonstrate any claims made for his 
goods.  $100.00 seemed like an inexpensive teaching
example.  Frankly, the visit was uncomfortable 
enough that I would not have wanted to visit
or insist upon an exchange or return for credit
of the subject printer.  We just had a dread
concerning the prospect of having to deal with 
the seller.  I am very glad it was only $100.00.
I can't say I would like to be in the Canadian's
shoes when having to deal with this seller.

What does all this add up to?  

1)  I am very inclined to believe the original
    poster, Dan when he wrote words to the 
    effect that "the machine had defective board".

2)  Let the buyer beware.



