James Lothian <simul8@simul8.demon.co.uk> writes:
> Um.. I don't think this is quite right. The RSC isn't a modem.
> As far as I can remember (I've got the manual for the RSC at
> home), it's a gadget that among other things allows a modem to 
> be shared between the diagnostic port and an ordinary serial port,
> and that provides some sort of serial line protocol (DDCMP?). 
> As you can tell, as far as I can remember is not far at all! However,
> I am quite sure that it isn't a modem. Anybody care to fill in the
> gaps?

  It's an "X" switch with additional bells and whistles. It has 4
serial ports:

  o console terminal
  o modem
  o host computer console port
  o host computer modem port

  In the normal mode, the console terminal would be hooked up to the host
computer's console port and the modem would be hooked up to the host com-
puter's modem port. Placing the box in service mode, the modem is hooked
up to the host computer's console port, the console terminal goes to mon-
itor mode (looking at the remote console traffic to/from the modem), and
the host computer's modem port is disconnected.

  In practice, almost nobody connected the host computer's modem port to
this box, because Field Service provided really lame 1200 or 2400 baud
modems 8-(

  The box has commands to turn on/off local copy to the console terminal
and some other stuff.

