Mark Greene <greenemj@my-deja.com> writes:
> We have since been told that we really should only be running 2 drives
> per bus; this of course immediatly begs the question: why, then, are
> there 5 slots in the chassie?

  With disks, if you can't get data to the drive in time, things just
wait 1/5400 of a second (or less on 7200 or 10K RPM drives). For a
streaming tape like DAT, if you can't get the data to the drive by the
time the drive is finished writing the previous block, the drive has
to stop, back up, and re-do the write. This process is called "shoe-
shining" (think about it) and creates excessive wear on both the drive
and the media. It also takes *forever* (compared to writing a block
normally). Some drives will write "pad" blocks if they are missing a
block or two instead of backing up. While this reduces shoeshining, it
reduces the tape capacity, since blocks that should have user data
instead have "this space for rent" markers 8-)

  However, DAT drives (at least the older ones, before DDS-3 and 125M
cartridges) don't have a particularly high head-to-media data rate. Un-
fortunately, many DAT drives (including the Seagate ones that I think
the DEC drives are based on) don't actually do full-speed Fast/Ultra
SCSI - they do between 6.6MB/sec and 7.8MB/sec, so they consume extra
bus bandwidth.

> I'd still like to hear what other people are doing for running backups.

  Either non-DEC DAT autoloaders (1 DDS-2 loader and 2 DDS-3 loaders, all
from Seagate) or DEC DLT robotic libraries (3 TL891's). The TL891's are
for sites that have a huge amount of data, frequent backups, or both. The
DAT loaders are for sites that just back up static data weekly.

