Hello, I have a Digital RoamAbout Access Point Bridge connected and up and
running.

I have some questions regarding it.  My initial reaction to this product is
that it does not conform to the definition of a bridge.  I understand a
bridge as a device that works at the datalink layer which can be used to
connect dissimilar networks.  In this case we have a wireless network and a
wired network.  The client stations are equipped with either a PCMCIA or ISA
card which uses Direct Sequence Spread Spectrum in 2.4GHz range.

It looks to me that the client stations (running either the DS/PC or DS/ISA
network adapters) do not conform to 802.11 standard.  In fact, if I sniff
the packets they send, they are standard ethernet packets.  I initially
thought that perhaps the bridge was receiving 802.11 packets, and it was
doing a frame conversion.  However, this does not seem to be the case.  It
is not even converting the frames (as far as I can tell) because they are
already ethernet.

This is my first experience using a bridge, so I may seem naive, but if a
bridge works at the datalink layer, and keeps a database of MAC addresses, I
think it should be able to forward a ping packet from a wireless host to a
wired host even if their network IP address are in different network
addressing schemes.

For instance --> wireless host is 192.168.0.1
                          wired host is 141.216.23.4

When I try to ping through the bridge, the bridge receives the packet but
does not forward it onto the wired link.  It would seem to me, that if it
worked at the link layer, it would use the MAC address of the destination
host, and not care what the IP scheme is.  Thus, it would broadcast the
packet onto the wired link.  However, test results show that it does not do
this.

If I then assign the wired host an address like:

192.168.0.2

The ping goes through correctly.  So, it only forwards packets destined for
an IP address in the same addressing scheme.  This is hardly what a bridge
is supposed to do.  There are two big limitations to this product: It does
not conform to 802.11 standards and it seems to do nothing more than pass
along a wireless ethernet packet to a wired ethernet link (and vice versa)
using IP addressing, and limiting the forward to only those packets destined
for the same network.

Does anyone have any experience with this product(s), such that they could
provide some insight on these questions?

Thanks in advance,

davis





